==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|lamentable}}, from {{der|en|la|lāmentābilis||full of sorrow, mournful; deplorable}}, from {{m|la|lāmentor||lament}}, from {{m|la|lāmenta||wailing, weeping}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ləˈmɛn.tə.bəl/|/ˈlæm.ən.tə.bəl/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Causing [[sorrow]], [[distress]] or [[regret]]; [[deplorable]], [[pitiful]] or [[distressing]].
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|1|II|iv|page=35|In what a '''lamentable''' caſe were I,<br>If Nature had not giuen me wiſedomes lore?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=Locomotive Running Past and Present|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=86|text=Moreover, in view of the '''lamentable''' present indecision of the Government in regard to electrification, the large-scale building of diesel power may prove to have been a farseeing move after all.}}

====Synonyms====
* See also [[Thesaurus:lamentable]]

